# Template for per-machine monitor layout.
# Copy to monitors.local.conf in this directory (that name is gitignored),
# then edit for the machine. config.conf sources it with source-optional,
# so installs without the file just fall back to the generic rule.
#
# Discover names, modes and scale with:  wlr-randr
#
# Gotchas:
#  - x/y are LOGICAL coords = physical size / scale. 3840 @ scale 1.5 = 2560,
#    so the second monitor starts at x:2560, not x:3840. Too large leaves a gap
#    the mouse cannot cross; too small overlaps the screens.
#  - repeat scale on every rule -- it is applied unconditionally and defaults
#    to 1.0, so omitting it snaps the panel back to unscaled.
#  - omit width/height/refresh to keep each panel's preferred mode. Identical
#    models can still differ here (e.g. 60.000 vs 59.997 Hz).
#  - give every connected output a rule; an unmatched monitor keeps its old
#    position.
#  - anchor names with ^...$ -- name is a regex, so DP-1 would also match DP-10.
#  - use positive coordinates only; negative ones break XWayland click handling.
#  - comments must start in column 0 -- the parser tests line[0] == '#'.
#
# Example: two 4K panels side by side, DisplayPort on the left.
monitorrule=name:^DP-1$,x:0,y:0,scale:1.50
monitorrule=name:^HDMI-A-1$,x:2560,y:0,scale:1.50
